# net.davidstrauss.taste.Fleet — one IDE window's fleet, as a read model.
#
# ENVIRONMENTS.md → "Shell integration rides a varlink interface". This is
# the substrate for the optional in-tree GNOME Shell extension (top-bar
# indicator + fleet popover) and for anything else that wants to watch a
# taste-ide window without being one.
#
# THE INTERFACE IS READ-ONLY BY DESIGN. No method here mutates anything,
# and none ever will under this name: starting a container, destroying an
# environment or answering a permission prompt are acts with consequences,
# and granting them to any process that can open a socket in the user's
# runtime directory is a decision nobody has made. A control interface, if
# it is ever wanted, arrives as a separately named interface with its own
# argument about authority.
#
# TRANSPORT. Standard varlink: NUL-terminated JSON messages over a unix
# stream socket, one socket per open workspace, named
# `taste-<workspace-key>-fleet.sock` in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (see
# `taste_core::environment::fleet_socket_path` for the exact search order,
# which differs inside a Flatpak sandbox). Mode 0600.
#
# VERSIONING. Varlink's IDL has no version syntax, so the version rides in
# every reply as `version`. It is the version of THIS read model, not of
# the IDE: a client checks it, and a client that does not recognise it
# should render nothing rather than guess. Adding an optional field is a
# compatible change and does not bump it; removing or retyping one does.
# Current version: 2 — v2 added `openIssues` to both replies, and is
# declared rather than silently compatible because a client that renders a
# fleet without the issue queue is now rendering half of it.
#
# `org.varlink.service` is also served on this socket — `GetInfo` names the
# workspace this window has open, `GetInterfaceDescription` returns this
# file verbatim — so a client can discover everything from the connection.

interface net.davidstrauss.taste.Fleet

# What one environment has spent through the IDE's auth proxy.
#
# The fleet's fuel gauge, and shared with the user's own interactive quota:
# every token here came out of the same subscription the user spends when
# they talk to an agent themselves. There is deliberately no limit or
# percentage field — the proxy records spend and does not enforce it, and
# the upstream API exposes no subscription ceiling, so a denominator here
# would be invented. Clients should render burn, not fullness.
type Spend (
  requests: int,
  inputTokens: int,
  outputTokens: int
)

# The chat working in an environment, as the fleet says it.
#
# More than one chat can be bound to one environment; `label` then names
# the first and counts the rest ("Claude 2 +1"), and `busy` is true if any
# of them has a turn in flight.
#
# `orchestrator` marks the workspace's orchestrator chat — the one whose
# environment socket serves the orchestration tools. At most one row in a
# fleet carries it. Added after version 2 and deliberately without a bump:
# a client that ignores it still renders a correct fleet, which is the
# stated test for a compatible addition.
type Chat (
  label: string,
  busy: bool,
  orchestrator: bool
)

# One environment.
type Row (
  # The slug — stable identity, and what every derived podman name is
  # built from. Never changes; `name` does.
  environment: string,
  # What to call it: the name the user gave it, else the slug.
  name: string,
  # Whether `name` came from the user.
  named: bool,
  # The environment backing the user's own checkout. Exactly one row has
  # this set, it always exists, and it can never be destroyed.
  primary: bool,
  # "container" or "safe". Container mode is the only real working mode;
  # everything else — including a container still building — is safe mode.
  mode: string,
  # The container lifecycle state, as one of: no-config, config-detected,
  # building, starting, running, failed, stopped.
  state: string,
  # `mode` and `state` in words, ready to render ("container mode ·
  # running · needs rebuild"). Prefer this to re-composing the two.
  detail: string,
  # The devcontainer config on disk no longer matches the running
  # container. The IDE will not apply it without asking the user.
  pendingRebuild: bool,
  chat: ?Chat,
  # The checked-out branch of this environment's own clone.
  branch: ?string,
  # Commits in this environment's clone the user's checkout has never
  # seen, and files not committed at all. Two different facts; never add
  # them together. Absent from the model until the git pass has run, in
  # which case both read 0 — see `gitKnown`.
  unpublished: int,
  dirty: int,
  # True once the off-thread git pass has filled `branch`, `unpublished`
  # and `dirty` for this row. While false those fields are unknown, not
  # zero.
  gitKnown: bool,
  # `agents/<environment>/*` branches waiting in the user's checkout: what
  # this environment has handed over for review.
  published: int,
  # Live shells attached to this environment — the user's terminals, the
  # agent's, `ide_exec` jobs, and the build's own lifecycle stream. The
  # monitor's answer to "is anything happening in there".
  shells: int,
  # Checkout plus volumes. Absent unless someone asked for the footprint:
  # it is a directory walk, and never a side effect of rendering.
  diskBytes: ?int,
  spend: Spend
)

# The fleet, once.
method List() -> (
  # This read model's version. Currently 2.
  version: int,
  # The open workspace's directory name — what the window is titled.
  workspace: string,
  # Branches waiting in the user's review inbox, across every environment.
  # The same number as the rows' `published` summed, said once.
  inbox: int,
  # Every environment's spend, added up. The gauge's needle.
  spend: Spend,
  # Open issues on the workspace's queue (refs/taste/issues) — work the
  # user or an agent has written down and nobody has finished. Not a sum
  # over the rows: issues belong to the workspace, and an unclaimed one
  # belongs to no environment at all.
  openIssues: int,
  # Primary first, then by the name the user reads.
  rows: []Row
)

# The fleet, and every subsequent change to it.
#
# Must be called with the `more` flag; without it the service replies
# `org.varlink.service.ExpectedMore`. The first reply is the current fleet
# — a client needs no `List` beforehand — and each later reply is the
# whole fleet again, not a delta: the model is small, and a client that
# has to reassemble deltas is a client that can disagree with the IDE
# about what an environment is called.
#
# Updates are driven off the IDE's own environment events and are
# coalesced: a client too slow to keep up sees the latest fleet, never a
# backlog of stale ones. The stream has no end — it stops when the client
# closes the connection, or when the window does.
method Watch() -> (
  version: int,
  workspace: string,
  inbox: int,
  spend: Spend,
  openIssues: int,
  rows: []Row
)
